<!-- PROBLEM -->
<section class="sec band-alt" id="problem">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
      <h2 class="h2">A canteen cooks for a guess, then pays for the difference</h2>
      <p class="lead">Hundreds or thousands of students eat in a college canteen every day. A large share of the food prepared goes unsold, gets over-served, or ends up as plate waste. That makes it an environmental problem and a social one.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="problem-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="ledger-title">Why it happens</p>
        <ul class="ledger">
          <li><b>Uncertain daily demand</b><span>Attendance and timetables change how many students arrive each day.</span></li>
          <li><b>Fixed-quantity cooking</b><span>The same batch is cooked whatever the day brings.</span></li>
          <li><b>Oversized portions</b><span>Plates are filled the same for everyone, and the leftovers go in the bin.</span></li>
          <li><b>No tracking</b><span>Nobody knows how much is wasted, at which meal, or from which dish.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="ledger-title">What it costs</p>
        <ul class="ledger">
          <li><b>Water, energy and labour</b><span>Every wasted plate carries the resources that went into cooking it.</span></li>
          <li><b>Money</b><span>The canteen pays for ingredients that are never eaten.</span></li>
          <li><b>Methane</b><span>Food that decomposes in mixed waste releases a potent greenhouse gas.</span></li>
          <li><b>Hunger nearby</b><span>Many people in neighbouring communities do not get enough to eat.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="stats" aria-label="Global food waste figures">
      <div class="stat"><b>1.05 bn t</b><span>of food was wasted worldwide in 2022</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>19%</b><span>of the food available to consumers was wasted</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>290 m t</b><span>of that came from the food service sector</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>783 m</b><span>people faced hunger in the same period</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="src">Source: UNEP Food Waste Index Report 2024, as reported by <a href="https://news.un.org/en/node/1148036" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UN News</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SOLUTION -->
<section class="sec" id="solution">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">The solution</p>
      <h2 class="h2">One loop: predict, cook, weigh, recover</h2>
      <p class="lead">The system joins demand forecasting, IoT-based waste monitoring and surplus redistribution. Each day's waste data makes the next day's forecast better.</p>
    </header>
    <ol class="loop">
      <li><span class="n">1</span><h3>Pre-select</h3><p>Students choose meals or opt out in a mobile or web app.</p><span class="tag"><i></i>Demand forecasting</span></li>
      <li><span class="n">2</span><h3>Forecast</h3><p>Opt-outs join past consumption, attendance and timetable patterns to predict demand.</p><span class="tag"><i></i>Demand forecasting</span></li>
      <li><span class="n">3</span><h3>Cook to plan</h3><p>The kitchen cooks what is needed, with portion control built into the menu.</p><span class="tag"><i></i>Demand forecasting</span></li>
      <li><span class="n">4</span><h3>Weigh</h3><p>Smart bins record how much is wasted, at which meal and from which dish.</p><span class="tag m"><i></i>IoT monitoring</span></li>
      <li><span class="n">5</span><h3>Recover</h3><p>Safe surplus goes to partner NGOs or food banks. Scraps go to compost or biogas.</p><span class="tag r"><i></i>Redistribution</span></li>
    </ol>
    <div class="return">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 12a9 9 0 0 1 15.5-6.2L21 8"/><path d="M21 3v5h-5"/><path d="M21 12a9 9 0 0 1-15.5 6.2L3 16"/><path d="M3 21v-5h5"/></svg>
      <p>The dashboard turns bin readings into insights for menu planning and portion control. Those insights flow back into step 1, so tomorrow's plan starts from what was actually wasted today.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

